In this lesson, we'll look at how to write numbers in scientific notation, a neat way to handle really big or really small numbers without all the zeros. The idea is simple. Take your number and slide the decimal point until exactly one non-zero digit sits right before it. That leading digit, plus everything after the decimal, becomes your number between one and ten. Next, count how many places you moved the decimal. That count is your exponent, and it tells you how many times you multiply or divide by ten. Here's the easy trick. If you shifted the decimal to the left, that means the original number was bigger, so your exponent is positive. If you shifted it to the right, the original number was smaller than one, so your exponent is negative. For example, forty-five hundred becomes four point five times ten to the three, since the decimal moved three places left. And zero point zero zero three two becomes three point two times ten to the negative three, since it moved three places right. Now let's see it in action.